<!-- ============ WHAT ENTERPRISE MEANS ============ -->
<section class="ac-blue sunk section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">What Enterprise Means</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Six things a small-business POS was never asked to do.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mini-grid">
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-building-store"></i></div><h3>Run a fleet, not a store</h3><p>Configuration, pricing and promotions set once and pushed to hundreds of locations, with a new store opened in hours rather than as a project.</p></div>
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-world"></i></div><h3>Trade in several countries</h3><p>Each market has its own fiscal rules, receipts, currencies and tax. They belong in the platform, not in a local vendor bolted on per country.</p></div>
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-scan"></i></div><h3>Know the stock, item by item</h3><p>Inventory accurate enough to sell store stock online. In apparel that means RFID, and RFID that is part of the system rather than middleware.</p></div>
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-truck-delivery"></i></div><h3>Fill orders from anywhere</h3><p>Ship from store, buy online pick up in store, endless aisle, returns to any location, on the same stock count the registers sell from.</p></div>
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-plug-connected"></i></div><h3>Fit the systems you already run</h3><p>ERP, e-commerce, payments, tax, shipping and marketing stay. The POS has to connect to them through open APIs, not replace them.</p></div>
      <div class="mini-card"><div class="mini-ico"><i class="ti ti-wifi-off"></i></div><h3>Keep selling when the network drops</h3><p>The app and its data on the device, so a Saturday outage is a sync later, not a closed store.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ THREE KINDS OF POS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="compare">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">The Three Kinds of POS</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Small-business cloud POS, legacy enterprise POS, or a unified commerce platform.</h2>
      <p class="section-sub">The categories describe how products in each group are typically built, not any one vendor. The last column is Teamwork Commerce, stated as fact and sourced on this site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="capx-wrap">
      <table class="capx eps-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Requirement</th><th scope="col">Small-business cloud POS</th><th scope="col">Legacy enterprise POS</th><th scope="col" class="eps-us-h">Unified commerce platform (Teamwork Commerce)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Devices on the floor</th><td>Built around a tablet or the vendor&#x27;s own terminal. Fine for a few registers.</td><td>Fixed registers, often Windows-based, with mobile added later as a separate app.</td><td class="eps-us">Native to iOS on iPhone and iPad from one app. Tap to Pay on iPhone. 30,000+ terminals live.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">When the network drops</th><td>Varies. Many run in a browser and stop, or queue a limited set of actions.</td><td>Usually keeps selling. The reconciliation work lives in the sync back.</td><td class="eps-us">The app and its data are on the device. Sells through the outage and syncs when the connection returns.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">More than one country</th><td>Typically one country or region per account.</td><td>Supported, usually through a local vendor per market.</td><td class="eps-us">Fiscal compliance in the platform across 40+ countries: NF525, TSE, RT printers, SII, CFDI, Japanese official receipts.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Inventory and RFID</th><td>Barcode counts. RFID through a third-party app.</td><td>RFID through middleware from another vendor.</td><td class="eps-us">RFID native: commissioning, transfers, counts from the POS handheld, self-checkout. 99.9% accuracy.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Online orders filled from stores</th><td>Basic ship-from-store, or a separate app.</td><td>A separate order management system, integrated.</td><td class="eps-us">Order management in the platform: 39+ omnichannel workflows with configurable order fill logic.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Connecting your other systems</th><td>An app marketplace.</td><td>A custom project per system.</td><td class="eps-us">Open APIs and 100+ integrations, live with SAP, Oracle, NetSuite and Microsoft Dynamics.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Reporting</th><td>Dashboards per store.</td><td>A data warehouse project.</td><td class="eps-us">150+ reports and Looker dashboards on the live transaction data.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Getting live</th><td>Self-serve.</td><td>Multi-quarter programs are common.</td><td class="eps-us">As fast as 120 days, led by Teamwork&#x27;s own service team.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin: 18px 0 0; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--text-faint);">Figures in the last column: 30,000+ terminals, 40+ countries, 99.9% RFID accuracy, 100+ integrations and 150+ reports are the platform's published numbers. "As fast as 120 days" is the fastest implementations run; yours depends on fleet size, complexity and integrations.</p>
  </div>
</section>

