<!-- ============ FAQ (visible content matches FAQ schema) ============ -->
<section class="ac-royal sunk section" style="padding-bottom: 80px;">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-eyebrow">Frequently Asked Questions</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Enterprise POS questions, answered.</h2>
    </div>
    <div style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          What is the best POS system for enterprise retail?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>The best enterprise POS is the one that runs your whole fleet on one system: every store, every country, every channel, with inventory, orders and fiscal compliance in the platform rather than bolted on. Judge candidates on a live pilot, not a demo deck. Teamwork Commerce is built for that profile: 30,000+ terminals across 40+ countries, native to iOS on iPhone and iPad, with RFID, order management and analytics on the same data as the POS. Retailers running it include ASICS, Moose Knuckles, Lammle's, Petco Mexico, FAO Schwarz and the Colorado Rockies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Is Teamwork Commerce a good POS for a multi-store apparel or footwear retailer?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. Fashion and footwear are where most of its fleet runs. Lammle's Western Wear operates 26 stores and eleven Calgary Stampede pop-ups on it, ASICS runs it across Europe and the Americas, and Princesse Tam Tam and Comptoir des Cotonniers run it across France, Italy, Spain, Germany, Belgium and Switzerland. Size runs, high return rates and seasonal pop-ups are handled by RFID inventory, cross-channel returns and a POS that opens a new location in hours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          What POS do luxury retailers use?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Luxury retailers on Teamwork Commerce include Moose Knuckles, Prada Shoes, Acne Studios, The Row and N.Peal. The things luxury floors ask for are in the platform: clienteling on one customer record across countries, RFID serialization for limited pieces, a cash drawer kept back of house so there is no cash wrap in the salon, and fiscal compliance in 40+ countries so Milan, London and New York run the same system.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Which POS runs on iPad and iPhone at enterprise scale?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Teamwork Commerce is native to iOS and runs on iPad at the counter and iPhone in an associate's hand, from one app and one cart. It accepts Tap to Pay on iPhone, keeps selling through a network outage because the app and its data are on the device, and is live on 30,000+ terminals. New associates are on a register in under 30 minutes because the interface follows the iOS patterns they already know.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          What is the difference between a POS and a unified commerce platform?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>A POS takes payment and records a sale. A unified commerce platform runs the POS, inventory, order management, customer records and reporting on one codebase and one data model, so a sale in a store, an online order shipped from that store and the stock count behind both are the same record. Teamwork Commerce is a unified commerce platform with the POS as its core: the other modules are extensions of the POS, not separate products integrated to it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Does Teamwork Commerce have native RFID?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes. RFID is part of the platform rather than middleware from another vendor: tag commissioning at receiving, transfers, cycle counts during opening hours from the mobile POS handheld, and RFID self-checkout that reads a whole basket at once. Retailers report 99.9% inventory accuracy, and the counts feed the same inventory the POS and the order management system sell from.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Can Teamwork Commerce replace a legacy enterprise POS, and how long does it take?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Yes, and implementations can go live in as fast as 120 days. How long yours takes depends on fleet size, operational complexity and how many integrations are in scope. Teamwork's own service team leads every implementation, working alongside any systems integrator you bring, and once a retailer is live, opening another store is a matter of hours rather than a project.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          Which enterprise POS integrates with SAP, Oracle, NetSuite and Microsoft Dynamics?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Teamwork Commerce has open APIs and live integrations with SAP, Oracle, NetSuite and Microsoft Dynamics, alongside 100+ integrations across e-commerce, payments, tax, shipping and marketing. The platform is processor-agnostic for payments. Integrations connect systems you already run; they do not replace what is native to the platform.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q"><button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false" onclick="var i=this.closest('.faq-item');i.classList.toggle('open');this.setAttribute('aria-expanded',i.classList.contains('open'))">
          How much does an enterprise POS system cost?
          <span class="faq-icon"><i class="ti ti-plus"></i></span>
        </button></h3>
        <div class="faq-answer"><p>Enterprise POS is priced on scope: the number of terminals and stores, the markets and their fiscal requirements, the modules in use and the integrations to build. Teamwork Commerce does not publish a price list. Pricing follows a discovery call that maps your stores, channels and systems, and the demo that comes after it runs on your own scenarios.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

